{{- /*
products.gohtml is the catalog list, optionally narrowed to one category.

The rows come from the cross-module read layer, not from a module's service: the
panel knows no module (ADR 0011). Every field is printed as an ordinary value,
so the template engine's escaping applies to all of them — a product title and a
category name are both operator-supplied text and the panel runs inside an
administrator's session.

Paging shows "next" only when the read returned one record more than the page
holds. There is no total and no last page: a count over a growing catalog is the
expensive half of paging and this screen does not need it.

THE FILTER TRAVELS IN THE ADDRESS, and the paging links carry it. A "next page"
that dropped the category would walk the reader out of the filtered view and
into the whole catalog without saying so — the same failure the sales report's
links avoid by carrying their period. It is appended CONDITIONALLY here, unlike
there: that report always covers some window, while this list is unfiltered most
of the time, and "&category=" hanging off every link would be noise in an
address bar operators read. If one did leak through, the handler trims the value
and an empty one is not a filter, so it would be harmless rather than a filter
by an identity nothing has.

THE FORM DOES NOT CARRY THE PAGE, on purpose. Choosing a category starts a new
listing, and page 7 of the unfiltered catalog names nothing in the filtered one;
submitting from there would land on an empty page of a category that has plenty.

THE CONTROL AND THE LIST MUST NEVER DISAGREE. Three states below say so out
loud, because in each of them the dropdown alone would be misleading: the
vocabulary could not be read, it was read and does not contain the chosen
identifier, or it was cut short so the identifier could not be checked. The one
thing this screen must not do is show an empty table under a plain "Products"
heading — an operator reads that as an empty catalog.
*/ -}}
{{ define "body" }}
<h1>Products</h1>

{{ with .CategoryFilter }}
{{ if .Options }}
<form method="get" action="{{ $.Path }}">
  <label for="category">Category</label>
  <select id="category" name="category">
    {{- /*
    The empty value is the way back to the whole catalog, and it is empty rather
    than a word like "all" because the handler treats an empty parameter as "no
    filter" — one spelling for "unfiltered" instead of two.
    */ -}}
    <option value="">All categories</option>
    {{- range .Options }}
    <option value="{{ .ID }}"{{ if .Selected }} selected{{ end }}>{{ .Name }}</option>
    {{- end }}
  </select>
  <button type="submit">Show</button>
</form>
{{ end }}

{{ if .Unavailable }}
{{- /*
The dropdown is absent because the category list could not be read. The page
still shows the products, since they were read successfully and a screen that
died over a control it could not fill would cost the operator the catalog too.
*/ -}}
<p class="muted">
  The category list could not be read, so the filter is not available.
  {{ if .Applied }}
  The products below are still narrowed to the category
  <code>{{ .ID }}</code>, which cannot be named or changed here —
  <a href="{{ $.Path }}">show all products</a>.
  {{ end }}
</p>
{{ else if .Unknown }}
<p class="muted">
  No category is known by the identifier <code>{{ .ID }}</code>. The list below
  is still narrowed by it, so what is under this line is what that filter
  matched and not the catalog —
  <a href="{{ $.Path }}">show all products</a>.
</p>
{{ else if .Unverified }}
<p class="muted">
  The category list is capped and was cut short, so <code>{{ .ID }}</code>
  could not be checked against it — it may well exist. The list below is
  narrowed by that identifier either way —
  <a href="{{ $.Path }}">show all products</a>.
</p>
{{ else if .Applied }}
<p class="muted">
  Narrowed to <strong>{{ .Name }}</strong> —
  <a href="{{ $.Path }}">show all products</a>.
</p>
{{ end }}
{{ end }}

{{ if .Products }}
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Title</th><th>Handle</th><th>Status</th><th>Updated</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
  {{ range .Products }}
    <tr>
      <td><a href="{{ $.Path }}/{{ .ID }}">{{ .Title }}</a></td>
      <td>{{ .Handle }}</td>
      <td>{{ .Status }}</td>
      <td>{{ if not .UpdatedAt.IsZero }}{{ .UpdatedAt.Format "2006-01-02 15:04" }}{{ end }}</td>
    </tr>
  {{ end }}
  </tbody>
</table>
{{ else }}
{{- /*
The empty message names the filter when there is one. "No products on this page"
under a category filter would be true and useless: it is the sentence an
operator reads as "the catalog is empty", and the one thing they would not then
check is the control that emptied it.
*/ -}}
{{ if .CategoryFilter.Applied }}
<p>No products in this category on this page.</p>
{{ else }}
<p>No products on this page.</p>
{{ end }}
{{ end }}

<nav>
  {{ if .HasPrev }}<a href="{{ .Path }}?page={{ .PrevPage }}{{ if .CategoryFilter.Applied }}&amp;category={{ .CategoryFilter.ID }}{{ end }}">Previous</a>{{ end }}
  <span>Page {{ .Page }}</span>
  {{ if .HasNext }}<a href="{{ .Path }}?page={{ .NextPage }}{{ if .CategoryFilter.Applied }}&amp;category={{ .CategoryFilter.ID }}{{ end }}">Next</a>{{ end }}
</nav>
{{ end }}
